This version uses the first row and column as notes, but the update pass runs from bottom to top. A separate `firstColumnZero` value handles the one marker that would otherwise share `matrix[0][0]`.

The reverse order matters because it keeps the marker row unchanged until each inner cell has checked it.

```java
class Solution {
    public void setZeroes(int[][] matrix) {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.length == 0 || matrix[0].length == 0) {
            return;
        }

        int rows = matrix.length;
        int cols = matrix[0].length;
        boolean firstColumnZero = false;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            if (matrix[row][0] == 0) {
                firstColumnZero = true;
            }

            for (int col = 1; col < cols; col++) {
                if (matrix[row][col] == 0) {
                    matrix[row][0] = 0;
                    matrix[0][col] = 0;
                }
            }
        }

        for (int row = rows - 1; row >= 0; row--) {
            for (int col = cols - 1; col >= 1; col--) {
                if (matrix[row][0] == 0 || matrix[0][col] == 0) {
                    matrix[row][col] = 0;
                }
            }

            if (firstColumnZero) {
                matrix[row][0] = 0;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Dry run with `[[1, 2, 3], [4, 0, 6]]`: the zero marks row `1` through `matrix[1][0]` and column `1` through `matrix[0][1]`. The reverse pass clears the inner cells in row `1` and column `1`, giving `[[1, 0, 3], [0, 0, 0]]`.

Time complexity: `O(m * n)`. Space complexity: `O(1)`. The method updates the supplied matrix and does not create another matrix or marker array.

Kept the marking separate from the update. Two boolean arrays remember the rows and columns that contain an original zero. This avoids treating a zero created during the second pass as a new source.

For a one-row or one-column matrix, the same checks still work without any special matrix logic.

