An empty matrix returns immediately, and a matrix with no zero keeps every value unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(m * n)` average time. Space complexity: `O(m + n)` in the worst case. The input matrix is updated in place during the second pass.

A copied matrix keeps the original values safe while the zero rows and columns are found. For every original zero, the matching row and column are cleared in `result`, and the result is copied back into `matrix`.

This is easy to follow, but it uses much more memory than the in-place versions.

